# example3d.scene - a known-good reference 3D scene every new project ships
# with. Load it to prove the 3D pipeline works before authoring your own:
#     Scene3D::Get().autoLoadScene = "example3d";
# (and make sure MyGUI::Update calls Scene3D::Get().Update(*game) every frame
# - the generated template already does; the engine does NOT drive it itself.)
# If this scene shows a lit ground plane with a box on it, your project's 3D
# stack (shaders, camera, per-frame update) is wired correctly. Then copy this
# file as a starting point, or delete it.
#
# THE CAMERA CONVENTION - the #1 new-3D-project gotcha. Getting it wrong
# renders a silent blank screen (the camera stares at empty space):
#   camera <name> <x> <y> <z> <pitch> <yaw>
#   - The engine looks along MINUS front: yaw 270 faces +Z, yaw 90 faces -Z.
#     So a yaw-270 camera must sit at SMALLER z than the content it films;
#     a yaw-90 camera at LARGER z.
#   - Visual down is +Y (the world is -Y-up): a camera above the ground sits
#     at NEGATIVE y and needs NEGATIVE pitch to look down at it.
# The camera below: 400 above the ground, 300 on the -Z side of content
# centered at z=300, facing +Z (yaw 270), tilted down 30 degrees (pitch -30).
# Working examples across projects follow this shape - if your scene is
# blank, check these two signs first.

ambient 0.35 0.35 0.38
light 0.5 1.0 0.25 1.0 0.9 0.85 0.8

camera main 0 -400 -300 -30 270

# Greybox content: a 600x600 ground plane (scaled x2) and a 100-unit box on
# it, using the 1-pixel white placeholder texture. `solid` gives the box a
# collision footprint for character movement.
model assets/models/common/plane.obj assets/editor/1pixel.png 0 0 300 0 2
model assets/models/common/box.obj assets/editor/1pixel.png 0 0 300 0 1 solid
